\title{Extremal Rigidity and Strict Growth of Finite\\Vietoris--Rips Clique Counts}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 15, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a simple graph on a finite vertex set of cardinality $n$, the clique complex is a subfamily of the power set and hence contains at most $2^n$ simplices. We establish the sharp equality characterization: the bound is attained if and only if the graph is complete. We also prove strict monotonicity under proper edge inclusion: adding at least one edge strictly increases the total number of cliques. These facts transfer directly to finite Vietoris--Rips complexes. For a symmetric dissimilarity $D$ whose diagonal lies below a scale $r$, the Vietoris--Rips complex equals the clique complex of the threshold proximity graph. Consequently, it has $2^n$ simplices exactly when every pair has dissimilarity at most $r$. Moreover, if $r\le s$ and a distinct pair satisfies $r<D(i,j)\le s$, then the simplex count at $s$ is strictly larger than at $r$. We present proofs, event-driven counting algorithms, examples, complexity analysis, applications, and quantitative directions suggested by the rigidity principle.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Vietoris--Rips complexes convert pairwise proximity data into combinatorial topology. Given finitely many objects and a scale parameter, one includes a simplex whenever all pairs of its vertices are sufficiently close. As the scale increases, the complexes form a filtration. This construction is central in topological data analysis because it requires only pairwise dissimilarities and because its clique-complex description permits graph-based computation.

The number of simplices is an elementary but consequential statistic. A complex on $n$ vertices may contain exponentially many simplices, affecting storage and downstream homological computations. The universal bound is $2^n$, the number of all subsets of the vertex set. We show that this bound has a rigid equality case and a strict dynamical form.

At graph level, every clique remains a clique after edges are added. If the inclusion is proper, a newly added edge is itself a new two-vertex clique. Thus total clique count is strictly increasing under proper edge inclusion. At the extremum, all $2^n$ subsets are cliques if and only if all two-element subsets are edges, equivalently if the graph is complete.

At geometric level, a symmetric dissimilarity at scale $r$ defines a graph whose edges are pairs at dissimilarity at most $r$. Under a natural diagonal condition, its clique complex is exactly the Vietoris--Rips complex. The graph statements imply that maximal simplex count characterizes universal proximity and that every edge birth forces a strict increase in total simplex count.

The argument needs neither the triangle inequality nor positivity: symmetry and diagonal control suffice. It therefore applies to finite metric spaces, pseudometrics, weighted undirected networks, and symmetric data-derived dissimilarities.

\section{Graphs, cliques, and finite complexes}
Let $V$ be a finite set with $|V|=n$. A \emph{simple graph} $G=(V,E)$ consists of unordered two-element subsets of $V$. The complete graph $K_V$ contains every such subset.

\begin{definition}[Clique and clique complex]
A subset $S\subseteq V$ is a clique of $G$ if every pair of distinct elements of $S$ is adjacent. The empty set and every singleton are cliques. The clique complex is
\[
\Cl(G)=\{S\subseteq V:S\text{ is a clique of }G\}.
\]
\end{definition}

This is an abstract simplicial complex, since every subset of a clique is again a clique.

\begin{lemma}[Power-set bound]
For every simple graph $G$ on an $n$-element vertex set,
\[
|\Cl(G)|\le 2^n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every clique is a subset of $V$, so $\Cl(G)\subseteq\mathcal P(V)$. The power set has cardinality $2^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Complete-graph complex]
For the complete graph,
\[
\Cl(K_V)=\mathcal P(V),\qquad |\Cl(K_V)|=2^n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every pair of distinct vertices is adjacent, so every subset satisfies the clique condition.
\end{proof}

\section{Extremal rigidity}
\begin{theorem}[Extremal rigidity of clique count]
Let $G$ be a simple graph on a finite vertex set $V$ with $|V|=n$. Then
\[
|\Cl(G)|=2^n
\]
if and only if $G=K_V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $G=K_V$, every subset is a clique. Conversely, assume $|\Cl(G)|=2^n$. The family $\Cl(G)$ is contained in $\mathcal P(V)$ and has the same finite cardinality, hence the families are equal. For any distinct $u,v\in V$, the set $\{u,v\}$ therefore lies in $\Cl(G)$. Thus $u$ and $v$ are adjacent. Every distinct pair is an edge, so $G=K_V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict extremal deficit]
If $G\ne K_V$, then $|\Cl(G)|<2^n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The power-set bound gives a weak inequality, while equality would imply completeness by the theorem.
\end{proof}

The proof shows how a global count recovers a local relation. Equality forces every subset to survive, including every pair, and the pairs determine the graph.

\section{Monotonicity under edge insertion}
For graphs on the same vertex set, write $G\subseteq H$ when every edge of $G$ is an edge of $H$.

\begin{lemma}[Clique-family monotonicity]
If $G\subseteq H$, then $\Cl(G)\subseteq\Cl(H)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every pair witnessing that a set is a clique of $G$ remains an edge of $H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict clique-count monotonicity]
If $G\subsetneq H$, then
\[
|\Cl(G)|<|\Cl(H)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Monotonicity gives inclusion of clique families. Proper edge inclusion supplies distinct $u,v$ for which $\{u,v\}$ is an edge of $H$ but not of $G$. Hence $\{u,v\}$ is in $\Cl(H)\setminus\Cl(G)$, so the family inclusion and therefore the cardinality inequality are strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exact single-edge jump]
Suppose $H$ is obtained from $G$ by adding one missing edge $uv$. Then
\[
|\Cl(H)|-|\Cl(G)|
=|\Cl(G[N_G(u)\cap N_G(v)])|,
\]
where the right side is the clique count in the graph induced by the common neighborhood of $u$ and $v$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every new clique contains both $u$ and $v$; otherwise it was already a clique of $G$. Removing $u,v$ leaves a clique among vertices adjacent to both endpoints. Conversely, adjoining $u,v$ to any clique in the common neighborhood gives a new clique of $H$. These operations are inverse bijections.
\end{proof}

The empty clique in the common neighborhood gives at least the newly inserted edge, recovering strict growth.

\section{Dissimilarities and Vietoris--Rips complexes}
Let $D:V\times V\to\mathbb R$ be symmetric, meaning $D(x,y)=D(y,x)$ for all $x,y$.

\begin{definition}[Threshold proximity graph]
At scale $r\in\mathbb R$, the graph $G_r(D)$ has vertex set $V$; distinct vertices $x,y$ are adjacent exactly when $D(x,y)\le r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Finite Vietoris--Rips complex]
The Vietoris--Rips complex at scale $r$ is
\[
\VR(D,r)=\{S\subseteq V: D(x,y)\le r\text{ for all }x,y\in S\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Because this definition includes equal pairs, assume the diagonal condition $D(x,x)\le r$ for every $x$. For a metric and nonnegative $r$, it is automatic.

\begin{theorem}[Clique representation]
If $D$ is symmetric and $D(x,x)\le r$ for every $x\in V$, then
\[
\VR(D,r)=\Cl(G_r(D)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $S\in\VR(D,r)$, then every distinct pair in $S$ has dissimilarity at most $r$ and is an edge, so $S$ is a clique. Conversely, if $S$ is a clique, distinct pairs satisfy the threshold by adjacency, while equal pairs satisfy it by the diagonal condition. Thus $S\in\VR(D,r)$.
\end{proof}

Symmetry gives an undirected graph, and diagonal control reconciles the all-pairs Rips condition with the distinct-pairs clique condition.

\section{Maximal size as a geometric certificate}
\begin{theorem}[Maximal Vietoris--Rips characterization]
Let $|V|=n$. If $D$ is symmetric and $D(x,x)\le r$ for every $x$, then
\[
|\VR(D,r)|=2^n
\]
if and only if $D(x,y)\le r$ for every $x,y\in V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The clique representation identifies $\VR(D,r)$ with $\Cl(G_r(D))$. Extremal rigidity says that this family has $2^n$ elements exactly when $G_r(D)$ is complete. Completeness supplies the inequality for distinct pairs, and the diagonal hypothesis supplies equal pairs. The converse follows in reverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Diameter threshold]
Let $(V,d)$ be a nonempty finite metric space with $|V|=n$, and let
\[
\diam(V)=\max_{x,y\in V}d(x,y).
\]
For every $r\ge0$,
\[
|\VR(d,r)|=2^n
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
r\ge\diam(V).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The theorem reduces maximality to $d(x,y)\le r$ for every pair, equivalent in a finite space to the maximum pairwise distance being at most $r$.
\end{proof}

Thus the saturation scale is the diameter, a stronger threshold than graph connectivity.

\section{Strict growth at edge births}
\begin{theorem}[Strict simplex growth at an edge birth]
Let $D$ be symmetric, let $r\le s$, and assume $D(x,x)\le r$ for all $x\in V$. If distinct $i,j\in V$ satisfy
\[
r<D(i,j)\le s,
\]
then
\[
|\VR(D,r)|<|\VR(D,s)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Threshold monotonicity gives $G_r(D)\subseteq G_s(D)$. The edge $ij$ is absent from the first graph and present in the second, so the inclusion is proper. Strict clique-count monotonicity applies. Since diagonal control at $r$ implies diagonal control at $s$, the clique representation identifies both counts with their Vietoris--Rips counts.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Critical values]
The function $N(r)=|\VR(D,r)|$ is nondecreasing and piecewise constant. If $(r,s]$ contains $D(i,j)$ for a distinct pair, then $N(r)<N(s)$. If it contains no off-diagonal dissimilarity value, then $N(r)=N(s)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The strict assertion is the theorem. If no value lies in the interval, each distinct pair passes the threshold at $r$ exactly when it passes at $s$, so the proximity graphs coincide.
\end{proof}

\section{Examples}
\begin{example}[Three points]
Suppose the three pairwise distances are $1,2,3$. For $0\le r<1$, the empty simplex and three vertices give count $4$. At scales $1$ and $2$, one edge appears each time, producing counts $5$ and $6$. At $3$, the last edge and the filled triangle appear together, giving $8=2^3$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Unit square]
For four corners of a unit square, four distances equal $1$ and two equal $\sqrt2$. Below $1$, the count is $5$. At $1$, four side edges give count $9$. At $\sqrt2$, the graph becomes complete and the count becomes $16$. The final jump consists of two diagonals, four triangles, and one four-vertex simplex.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[One missing edge]
Delete one edge $uv$ from $K_V$, where $|V|=n$. A subset fails to be a clique exactly when it contains both endpoints. The other $n-2$ vertices are arbitrary, so the deficit is $2^{n-2}$ and
\[
|\Cl(G)|=2^n-2^{n-2}=3\cdot2^{n-2}.
\]
This shows that a single edge birth can create exponentially many simplices.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
\subsection{Exhaustive counting}
Given a symmetric $n\times n$ dissimilarity matrix and a scale $r$, build the threshold adjacency matrix, enumerate the $2^n$ bit masks, and accept a mask exactly when every selected unordered pair is adjacent. The construction takes $O(n^2)$ time. Direct enumeration takes $O(n^2 2^n)$ worst-case time and $O(n^2)$ auxiliary space. Correctness follows from the clique representation theorem.

\subsection{Saturation testing}
To decide whether the count is $2^n$, enumeration is unnecessary. Check all diagonal entries and all unordered pairs against $r$. The maximal Vietoris--Rips characterization proves that this quadratic test is exact. It uses $O(n^2)$ time and constant extra space beyond the input.

\subsection{Event-driven filtration profile}
Collect and sort distinct off-diagonal values. Begin below the smallest value with the edgeless graph, whose clique complex consists of the empty set and $n$ vertices. At each critical value, insert every edge of that weight, update the clique count, and record the jump. Sorting takes $O(n^2\log n)$ comparisons. Recomputing by exhaustive enumeration at every one of at most $\binom n2$ values gives a conservative $O(n^4 2^n)$ bound; incremental clique maintenance can be much faster. The critical-value corollary guarantees positive growth at every nonempty insertion event and constancy between events.

\section{Applications and discussion}
In a finite metric space, saturation detects the diameter. It supplies a consistency check and a stopping criterion once the fully saturated complex has lost geometric discrimination. In communication networks, clique simplices represent mutually communicating groups; maximality is equivalent to universal pairwise communication, and increasing range cannot add a link without changing the group family.

The result also forecasts computational complexity. Near the diameter threshold, explicit enumeration approaches the exact worst-case size $2^n$. Algorithms should use implicit representations, sparsification, or dimension truncation. If dimensions above $k$ are discarded, the complete-complex count becomes $\sum_{j=0}^{k+1}\binom nj$.

For approximate filtrations compared by scale shifts or multiplicative interleavings, saturation offers a rigid landmark. Near-saturation suggests a quantitative problem: infer missing-edge structure from the simplex deficit. Total count is nevertheless coarse; nonisomorphic graphs can share it, so strict growth detects an event without identifying it.

Missing-edge analysis clarifies the quantitative challenge. Every missing edge excludes all subsets containing its endpoints, but exclusions overlap. The deficit is an inclusion--exclusion quantity governed by the missing-edge graph. Matchings, stars, and general overlap patterns therefore lead to different counts even with the same number of absent edges.

\section{Future directions}
Five extensions are natural. First, exact defect bounds should relate $2^n-|\Cl(G)|$ to the missing-edge graph and characterize equality. Second, critical-scale decompositions should express each jump through cliques completed by newborn edges; the common-neighborhood formula handles a single edge, while simultaneous births require overlap control. Third, dimension-refined vectors should count $k$-simplices separately. Fourth, rigidity should be transferred through additive or multiplicative approximation guarantees. Finally, locally finite infinite filtrations may be approached through compatible finite-window exhaustions.

\section{Conclusion}
For finite graphs, $2^n$ is the maximal clique-complex size, and equality holds exactly for the complete graph. Proper edge inclusion strictly increases clique count. Through threshold graphs, these become geometric statements: a finite Vietoris--Rips complex has maximal size exactly when every pair lies within scale, and every edge birth forces strict simplex growth. Total simplex count is therefore both an extremal certificate and an event detector for finite proximity filtrations.

\end{document}

